    <DataNode elementId="AT1G10760-MONOMER" textLabel="glucan, water dikinase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SAC6" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="275" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">|FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER GWD| (glucan, water dikinase) is a monomeric protein found in the plastid that plays a crucial role in starch degradation |CITS: [19325165]|. By phosphorylating the hydroxyl group on the C6 carbon of glucosyl residues in |FRAME: Starch starch|, |FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER GWD| appears to cause structural changes in starch that put it in a transition state where the phosphoglucan chains become less ordered, though they are still insoluble |CITS: [19325165]|. These glucans can then served as good substrates for the downstream enzyme |FRAME: AT5G26570-MONOMER PWD| that promotes solubilization of the starch |CITS: [15618411][18419779][19325165]|. In &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; experiments, |FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER GWD| shows a preference for the model substrate crystalline |FRAME: Maltodextrins maltodextrin|, and has a specific activty of 56.3 +/- 4 nmol phosphate incorporated mg&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; protein min&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; with it. Crystalline |FRAME: Maltodextrins maltodextrin| adopts a B-type allomorph, also found in the starch of Arabidopsis leaves, potato leaves, and potato tubers, and starch in these tissues can be acted on by |FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER GWD|, but the level of activity is significantly reduced. The next highest level of activity, measured for Arabidopsis leaves that lack functional GWD, is only 11.2 +/- 1.4 nmol phosphate incorporated mg&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; protein min&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;|CITS: [18419779]|. Within glucan chains, this enzymes seems to prefer phosphorylating the hydroxyl group on the C6 carbon of glucose |CITS: [11487701][16914145]|. Analysis of the products formed by incubation of |FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER GWD| with crystalline |FRAME: Maltodextrins maltodextrin| indicate it can form mono-, di-, and triphosphate glucan chains with degrees of polymerization ranging from 10-37 residues |CITS: [18419779]|. GWD activity &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; is expected to be high during the night when transitory starch reserves created during photosynthesis in the leaf are broken down to form usable sugars, such as |FRAME: SUCROSE sucrose|. Consistent with this model, there is evidence that |FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER GWD| mRNA levels peak at the end of the light period, however, there is no evidence that the |FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER GWD| protein levels change throughout the day |CITS: [11487701]|. The biological activity of this enzyme &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; is supported by the phenotype of the &lt;i&gt;sex1&lt;/i&gt; GWD mutants that have reduced levels of glucose-6-phosphate in their starch. The elevated level of overall starch in these mutants highlights the importance of GWD for starch degradation |CITS: [11487701]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G10760-MONOMER" />
